<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.0: Lesson Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01689</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.0: Lesson Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0d33a5b1-d572-4631-b2bb-63ca6f97e47d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Lesson Narrative</title>
<para>This lesson challenges students to recognize polynomials of many different forms. After assessing the form of the polynomial, and with no other context, students will select an appropriate strategy for factoring the polynomial. They will then implement that strategy by factoring the polynomial down to its fully factored form, where all the factors are prime.</para>
<para>Additionally, students will analyze an expression to determine if it is prime or fully factored, or if it can be factored further.</para>

<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
    <caption>Learning Objectives</caption>
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Learning Targets (Student Facing)</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
<list>
  <item> Analyze a polynomial by its form, select an appropriate strategy for factoring, and correctly factor the polynomial down to its prime factors. </item>
</list>
 </entry>
        <entry>
<list>
  <item> Recognize and use the appropriate method to factor a polynomial completely. </item>
</list>
      </entry></row>
    </tbody>
  </table>

<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption>Texas Essential Knowledge and Skills (TEKS)</caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        TEKS
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Explanation of Coverage
      </entry>
    </row>  
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>A1(B) use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a plan or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution</para>
        </entry>
        <entry><para>Math process coverage: Lesson provides content that supports this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>A1(F) analyze mathematical relationships to connect and communicate mathematical ideas</para></entry>
        <entry><para>Math process coverage: Lesson provides content that supports this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
<entry> <para>A1(G) display, explain, and justify mathematical ideas and arguments using precise mathematical language in written or oral communication</para></entry>
<entry><para>Math process coverage: Lesson provides content that supports this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>A10(D) rewrite polynomial expressions of degree one and degree two in equivalent forms using the distributive property</para></entry>
        <entry><para>Full coverage: Lesson provides content that covers all parts of this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>A10(E) factor, if possible, trinomials with real factors in the form 
            \(ax^2 + bx + c\), including perfect square trinomials of degree two</para></entry>
          <entry><para>Full coverage: Lesson provides content that covers all parts of this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><para>A10(F) decide if a binomial can be written as the difference of two squares and, if possible, use the structure of a difference of two squares to rewrite the binomial</para></entry>
          <entry><para>Full coverage: Lesson provides content that covers all parts of this TEKS.</para></entry>
        </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>Lesson Activities</title>
<para>Here are the instructional activities for the lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item> 6.7.1: Factoring Strategies for Polynomials </item>
  <item> 6.7.2: Reviewing General Strategies to Factor a Polynomial </item>
  <list>
    <item> 6.7.2: Self Check </item>
    <item> 6.7.2: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 6.7.3: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring </item>
  <list>
    <item> 6.7.3: Self Check </item>
    <item> 6.7.3: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 6.7.4: Is the Polynomial Completely Factored? </item>
</list>
<para>Students will also complete a series of problems in the 6.7.5: Practice.</para>
<title>Required Materials</title>
<list>
  <item>Pencil and paper </item>
</list>
<title>Required Preparation</title>
<list>
  <item>None </item>
</list>
<title>Lesson Vocabulary</title>
<para>During this lesson, it is important to:</para>
<list>
  <item> Familiarize students with the vocabulary words they will see throughout the lesson. Encourage students to look for these words and notice their use and meanings. </item>
  <item> Encourage students to use key vocabulary words in “math talk” and their written and oral explanations. </item>
  <item> Utilize a word wall. Sample cards are located here: <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8ed3aa68763498713b44d3c2e537ffe61b4755f0">Mathematics Vocabulary Word Wall Cards</link> </item>
</list>
<para>Vocabulary words that are emphasized in this lesson:</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        Previous Vocabulary 
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        New Vocabulary
      </entry>
    </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <list>
          <item>conjugate pair </item>
          <item> difference of squares </item>
          <item> binomial </item>
          <item> trinomial </item>
          <item> greatest common factor (GCF) </item>
          <item> factoring </item>
        </list>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <list>
          <item>none</item>
        </list>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>To support newcomers or students identified at the beginning level of language proficiency, share the following Quizlet links to help students gain an understanding of the academic vocabulary. Use the Spanish versions to anchor student understanding before bridging to the English versions. </para>
<list>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881785388/raise-unit-6-spn-vocabulary-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jqt">Unit 6 Spanish Vocabulary</link></item>
  <item><link url="https://quizlet.com/881535297/raise-unit-6-vocabulary-flash-cards/?i=5eauv9&amp;x=1jqt">Unit 6 Vocabulary</link></item>
</list>
<title>Support for English Language Learners</title>
    <para>Throughout this lesson, activities are incorporated that align to the following ELPS. The suggested activities are only a sampling of the types of support and scaffolding that can extend the learning for English language learners. Continue to find additional opportunities as you build your own set of ELL learning routines. </para>
<list>
 <item>ELPS 2(F) listen to and derive meaning from a variety of media such as audiotape, video, DVD, and CD-ROM to build and reinforce concept and language attainment</item> 
 <item>ELPS 3(C) speak using a variety of grammatical structures, sentence lengths, sentence types, and connecting words with increasing accuracy and ease as more English is acquired</item>
 <item>ELPS 3(D) speak using grade-level content area vocabulary in context to internalize new English words and build academic language proficiency</item>
 <item>ELPS 3(E) share information in cooperative learning interactions</item>
 <item>ELPS 3(I) adapt spoken language appropriately for formal and informal purposes</item>
</list>

<title>Support for Building Character</title>
<para>Throughout this unit, find ways to encourage and support students to work on cultivating their <emphasis>judgment</emphasis>.</para>
<para>Here are some tips to try during this lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item> <link url="https://characterlab.org/tips-of-the-week/how-did-you-do/">How Did You Do?</link> </item>
  <item> <link url="https://characterlab.org/tips-of-the-week/through-the-eyes-of-a-child/">Through the Eyes of a Child</link> </item>
</list>
<para>You can find other tips located here in the <link url="https://characterlab.org/playbooks/judgment/">Playbook on Judgment</link> from Character Lab.</para></section>
